Build ONE self-contained HTML file: an interactive 3D "Atlas Globe" of a code base, drawn on a 2D <canvas> with plain JavaScript.
No libraries, no CDN, no network calls, no build step. It must work offline by double-clicking the file.
Build ONLY the graph and its UI. Do not analyse code, and do not add any tool buttons.

DATA (the file contains it as one constant so it is easy to replace):
const D = { planets:[{n:"folder/path", g:0, f:[fileIndex,...]}], groups:["name",...],
            files:[["path/file.py","python",linesOfCode,symbolCount,planetIndex],...],
            links:[[fileIndexA,fileIndexB,weight],...] };
planets = folders, files = files, links = imports/calls between two files (weight = how many).
Include a small made-up demo dataset (about 12 folders, 120 files, 200 links, generated with a seeded random so it is repeatable).
Item numbering: item i < nP is folder i; item nP + j is file j. Merge links of both directions into one (sum the weights).

WHAT IS DRAWN
- One globe of dust. Every folder and every file is ONE dot of the same size (radius 1 px) on the sphere surface. Nothing is nested.
- Folder dots are grey (#b4b8bf). File dots are coloured by language: python #4b8bbe, javascript #f1e05a, typescript #3178c6,
  bash #5fcf6a, go #00add8, rust #dea584, sql #f29111, swift #ff7a2f, tsx #2fa8e8, csharp #9b5de5, c #8a8f98, java #e76f51,
  powershell #2a6fdb, anything else #9aa4b2.
- Depth shading: alpha = 0.3 + 0.7 * (1 - (depth - nearest) / (farthest - nearest)), clamped 0.22..1, times a slow twinkle
  (0.86 + 0.14 * sin(time * 1.6 + index * 1.7)). When something is selected, dots that are not related are drawn at 20% of that.
- Background #17181c, text #cfe9df, monospace 12px. Also draw 1200 faint stars far away and 900 faint specks just outside the globe.
- Grid: 100 latitude circles and 100 meridians on the sphere (72 segments each), turning with the globe, width 0.4,
  alpha 0.13 on the near side and 0.05 on the far side. A checkbox switches it off.

LAYOUT (deterministic: use a seeded random, seed = 31102024, rand = (seed = (seed*1664525 + 1013904223) >>> 0) / 2^32)
1. Patch radius per folder: patchR = 3*sqrt(fileCount + 1) + 3. Globe radius R = sqrt(sum((patchR + 6)^2) * 1.7 / 4).
2. Place folder centres on the sphere with a Fibonacci spiral (golden angle 2.399963; z = 1 - 2*(k+0.5)/nP). Sort folders by file count
   (biggest first) and give them a randomly shuffled slot each.
3. Relax the folder positions (280 iterations, 120 if there are more than 600 folders), cooling step = 0.5*cool + 0.05, cool = 1 - it/iterations:
   - repel two folders whose angular distance is smaller than (angular patch radii sum + 0.03), strength 0.3 * overlap * step;
   - attract two LINKED folders (any link between their files) when they are farther apart than that, strength 0.02 * min(3, log2(1 + weight)) * step;
   - after each step put every position back on the unit sphere.
4. Each folder dot sits at its centre. Its files form a sunflower spiral around it, biggest by symbol count first:
   radius = 3*sqrt(k + 1.2) * (0.92 + rand*0.16) + 0.6*folderRadius, angle = k*2.399963 + rand*0.15,
   in the tangent plane of the sphere, then projected back onto the sphere surface.

CAMERA AND MOTION
- Perspective projection: focal length = min(width, height) * 1.1, camera starts at yaw 0.5, pitch 0.35, distance 1.25*R*2.6.
  The globe is tilted 0.41 rad and always pivots around its centre.
- The globe turns about once per 3 minutes (0.036 rad/s). The camera orbits slowly (about once per 9 minutes). Space pauses both.
- Drag = slide the picture. Alt+drag = rotate (with inertia, no limit on the angle). Mouse wheel = zoom (exponential, with min and max).
- Click (moved less than 4 px) on a dot = select it. Click on empty space = clear. Esc = clear.
- Selecting a dot turns the globe so the dot faces the camera and zooms to its patch. Selection can be given in the URL hash: #p12 (folder 12), #f34 (file 34).
- Hover a dot (within 5 px, nearest one wins): ring around it and a tooltip. Folder: "name / folder / N files / N linked folders".
  File: "path / language / N symbols / N links".

LINKS
- Draw each link as an arc lifted above the surface: take the midpoint of the two world positions, push it out to R * lift
  (lift = min(1.5, 1 + (1 - |mid|/R) * 1.6)), project it, and use control = 2*projectedMid - (screenA + screenB)/2 for a quadratic curve.
- Links are ON by default: mint rgba(120,226,190,0.26), width 0.9. While something is selected the other links drop to alpha 0.05
  and the selected item's own links (for a folder: links of any of its files) are drawn in white rgba(228,230,234,0.8).
- LIGHTS (checkbox "link lights", on by default; off at start if prefers-reduced-motion):
  every link always carries one light. It travels from the first file to the second, then its rail runs on into the second file
  (it drains) while a ring spreads out and fades around the second file (radius 2 to 11 px, 4 fade levels), then it starts again.
  Speed factor LIGHT_SPEED = 0.05; one cycle per link = (3.4 + (linkIndex % 7) * 0.35) / LIGHT_SPEED seconds, so a light crosses a link in about 27 to 44 s;
  phase = linkIndex * 0.618 so the links are never in step.
  The rail is 150 px long on the screen for EVERY link (a shorter link gets a rail as long as itself), 100000 steps, drawn as 12 sections
  that fade (alpha quadratic) and thin out behind the front. Each section is an exact piece of the quadratic curve (a piece of a
  quadratic Bezier between t=a and t=b is a quadratic with control (1-a)(1-b)*A + ((1-a)b + a(1-b))*C + ab*B).
  There is NO bright dot at the front. Rails are mint rgba(120,226,190) with normal blending; links on the far side of the globe are dimmer (x0.4);
  the selected item's rails are white and brighter (additive blending). Batch all rails of one fade level into ONE path and stroke it once.
- Dots on the far side of the globe are still drawn (dim). Links with an end behind the camera are skipped.

UI (a panel on the right, 350 px wide, HIDDEN by default)
- A button "< Show panel" fixed at the top right, and "Hide panel >" inside the panel. Key P toggles. Use aria-controls and aria-expanded.
  The canvas takes the whole width while the panel is hidden.
- Panel contents, top to bottom: title "<name> - Atlas Globe"; a stats line ("N folders and N files as dust on one globe, N links. Offline, nothing is sent anywhere.");
  a search box (starts at 2 characters, up to 6 folders then up to 14 files, Enter picks the first); checkboxes: rotation, camera orbit,
  links, link lights, grid lines, labels (key L); a "Reset view" button; an info card for the selection; a short "How to explore" text.
- Info card for a file: path, language, lines, symbols, link to its folder, number of links, and its 14 strongest neighbours (name + weight, click to jump).
  For a folder: name, file count, top 5 languages, number of linked folders, and its 12 strongest linked folders (click to jump).
- Legend at the bottom left: "Dot colour = language", a grey "folder" entry and one entry per language with its file count (largest first).
- Labels (off by default): folder names, only for folders on the side facing us and big enough on screen (patch radius > 26 px), 11px;
  the selected item's name is always shown in white, 12px.
- Escape every file name and path before putting it into HTML (they are untrusted text).

PERFORMANCE
- Typed arrays (Float32Array) for positions and screen coordinates, no per-frame allocations in the dot loop,
  one path for all link lines, one stroke per rail fade level, device pixel ratio capped at 1.5, frame time step capped at 0.05 s.
- It must stay smooth with about 270 folders, 3700 files and 3500 links.

ACCEPTANCE CHECKS (run them and tell me the result)
1. The file opens offline with no console error and no network request.
2. The same data always gives the same picture (seeded layout).
3. Every link has a light and a ring appears at the second file when it arrives; no two neighbouring links move in step.
4. All rails have the same on-screen length (150 px) on a long and on a short link; very short links get a shorter rail.
5. Click a file: only its links stay bright (white), others dim, the info card lists its neighbours, Esc clears.
6. The panel is hidden at first; the button and the P key show and hide it; the canvas resizes.
7. With "links" off, selecting a dot still shows that dot's own links.
